1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,582
Ask anyone what the nineteen fifties were

2
00:00:02,582 --> 00:00:05,038
like, and you'll hear the same picture.

3
00:00:05,218 --> 00:00:06,855
A white picket fence.

4
00:00:07,035 --> 00:00:08,805
A mother in pearls,

5
00:00:08,805 --> 00:00:11,041
vacuuming in high heels.

6
00:00:11,221 --> 00:00:12,884
A father in a suit,

7
00:00:12,884 --> 00:00:14,634
home at five-thirty.

8
00:00:14,814 --> 00:00:16,568
Two perfect children,

9
00:00:16,568 --> 00:00:18,488
a dog, and a new car in

10
00:00:18,488 --> 00:00:19,574
the driveway.

11
00:00:19,754 --> 00:00:20,969
Everyone was happy,

12
00:00:20,969 --> 00:00:22,120
everyone was rich,

13
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,910
and nothing ever went wrong.

14
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:26,327
Some of that picture is true.

15
00:00:26,507 --> 00:00:27,668
A lot of it isn't.

16
00:00:27,848 --> 00:00:29,726
Much of what we think we know

17
00:00:29,726 --> 00:00:32,640
about the fifties comes from television shows

18
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:35,295
and advertisements that were made to sell

19
00:00:35,295 --> 00:00:35,813
a dream.

20
00:00:35,993 --> 00:00:38,741
The real decade was more interesting,

21
00:00:38,741 --> 00:00:41,341
more complicated, and in some ways,

22
00:00:41,341 --> 00:00:42,900
much more surprising.

23
00:00:43,251 --> 00:00:46,180
So today, let's take the biggest myths

24
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:48,571
about nineteen fifties America,

25
00:00:48,571 --> 00:00:49,419
one by one,

26
00:00:49,419 --> 00:00:51,500
and hold each of them up to

27
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:52,271
the facts.

28
00:00:52,451 --> 00:00:54,425
Some of them will surprise you.

29
00:00:54,605 --> 00:00:56,539
A few of them might even change

30
00:00:56,539 --> 00:00:58,785
how you remember your own childhood.

31
00:00:59,135 --> 00:01:00,980
But first, a quick thought.

32
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:03,350
If you lived through the fifties,

33
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:05,340
you already know some of this.

34
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,335
You remember things the history books leave

35
00:01:08,335 --> 00:01:08,597
out.

36
00:01:08,777 --> 00:01:09,645
So as we go,

37
00:01:09,645 --> 00:01:12,756
ask yourself whether each myth matches what

38
00:01:12,756 --> 00:01:14,710
you saw with your own eyes,

39
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:15,940
in your own town,

40
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:17,821
at your own kitchen table.

41
00:01:18,171 --> 00:01:20,476
The first myth is that everyone in

42
00:01:20,476 --> 00:01:22,645
the fifties lived in a new house

43
00:01:22,645 --> 00:01:23,662
in the suburbs.

44
00:01:23,842 --> 00:01:26,001
It's true that the suburbs exploded.

45
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:29,358
Places like Levittown went up almost overnight,

46
00:01:29,358 --> 00:01:32,062
and millions of young families moved out

47
00:01:32,062 --> 00:01:33,008
of the cities.

48
00:01:33,188 --> 00:01:35,472
It was one of the biggest migrations

49
00:01:35,472 --> 00:01:36,741
in American history.

50
00:01:37,091 --> 00:01:39,796
The first Levittown houses sold for under

51
00:01:39,796 --> 00:01:41,314
eight thousand dollars,

52
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:42,303
and with the G.

53
00:01:42,483 --> 00:01:42,774
I.

54
00:01:42,954 --> 00:01:45,991
Bill, a returning veteran could often move

55
00:01:45,991 --> 00:01:48,016
in with almost nothing down.

56
00:01:48,196 --> 00:01:50,147
For a young couple who had grown

57
00:01:50,147 --> 00:01:51,427
up in the Depression,

58
00:01:51,427 --> 00:01:53,135
a small house with a yard of

59
00:01:53,135 --> 00:01:54,964
their own felt like a miracle.

60
00:01:55,144 --> 00:01:57,464
That part of the dream was very

61
00:01:57,464 --> 00:01:57,838
real.

62
00:01:58,188 --> 00:01:59,868
But in nineteen sixty,

63
00:01:59,868 --> 00:02:02,922
after a whole decade of suburban growth,

64
00:02:02,922 --> 00:02:05,900
only about one in three Americans lived

65
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:07,046
in the suburbs.

66
00:02:07,226 --> 00:02:09,502
The rest lived in big cities,

67
00:02:09,502 --> 00:02:11,464
small towns and on farms.

68
00:02:11,644 --> 00:02:14,885
Millions still lived in old apartment buildings

69
00:02:14,885 --> 00:02:16,954
in crowded city neighborhoods,

70
00:02:16,954 --> 00:02:19,782
or in farmhouses without indoor plumbing.

71
00:02:20,133 --> 00:02:22,123
In fact, in nineteen fifty,

72
00:02:22,123 --> 00:02:24,850
about a third of American homes still

73
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:27,725
had no complete indoor plumbing at all.

74
00:02:27,905 --> 00:02:30,056
No bathtub or shower,

75
00:02:30,056 --> 00:02:32,002
or no flush toilet,

76
00:02:32,002 --> 00:02:33,641
or no hot water.

77
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:35,903
In the rural South and in parts

78
00:02:35,903 --> 00:02:37,179
of the countryside,

79
00:02:37,179 --> 00:02:39,731
the outhouse in the backyard was still

80
00:02:39,731 --> 00:02:41,746
a fact of daily life well into

81
00:02:41,746 --> 00:02:42,551
the fifties.

82
00:02:42,901 --> 00:02:45,145
The second myth is that mothers in

83
00:02:45,145 --> 00:02:47,917
the fifties never worked outside the home.

84
00:02:48,097 --> 00:02:49,931
The television mothers didn't.

85
00:02:50,111 --> 00:02:52,758
June Cleaver never went to an office.

86
00:02:52,938 --> 00:02:55,366
But the real numbers tell a different

87
00:02:55,366 --> 00:02:55,760
story.

88
00:02:56,109 --> 00:02:57,700
In the nineteen fifties,

89
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:00,350
about one in three American women worked

90
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:02,602
for pay, and the number was rising

91
00:03:02,602 --> 00:03:03,331
every year.

92
00:03:03,511 --> 00:03:04,964
By nineteen sixty,

93
00:03:04,964 --> 00:03:08,355
around thirty percent of married women had

94
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:08,759
jobs.

95
00:03:08,939 --> 00:03:10,783
They were secretaries,

96
00:03:10,783 --> 00:03:13,633
nurses, teachers, factory workers,

97
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:17,658
telephone operators, waitresses and shop clerks.

98
00:03:18,007 --> 00:03:20,530
Many of those women had already proven

99
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:22,257
themselves during the war.

100
00:03:22,437 --> 00:03:24,229
Millions had built ships,

101
00:03:24,229 --> 00:03:26,737
planes and tanks while the men were

102
00:03:26,737 --> 00:03:27,383
overseas.

103
00:03:27,563 --> 00:03:29,275
When the soldiers came home,

104
00:03:29,275 --> 00:03:30,805
a lot of them were let go

105
00:03:30,805 --> 00:03:33,251
to make room for the returning veterans.

106
00:03:33,431 --> 00:03:35,708
But many never forgot what they had

107
00:03:35,708 --> 00:03:37,985
done, and plenty of them went right

108
00:03:37,985 --> 00:03:38,830
back to work.

109
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,368
Many of them went back to work

110
00:03:41,368 --> 00:03:43,921
once their children were in school,

111
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:45,817
to help pay for the house,

112
00:03:45,817 --> 00:03:47,203
the car or college.

113
00:03:47,383 --> 00:03:48,962
And for poor families,

114
00:03:48,962 --> 00:03:51,690
immigrant families and Black families,

115
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:54,848
a mother's paycheck had never been optional.

116
00:03:55,028 --> 00:03:57,247
The stay-at-home mother was real,

117
00:03:57,247 --> 00:03:59,533
but she was never the whole story.

118
00:03:59,882 --> 00:04:02,247
The third myth is that everyone in

119
00:04:02,247 --> 00:04:04,194
the fifties was comfortable,

120
00:04:04,194 --> 00:04:06,141
with a good job and money to

121
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:06,558
spare.

122
00:04:06,738 --> 00:04:08,131
It was a boom decade.

123
00:04:08,311 --> 00:04:09,647
Wages rose.

124
00:04:09,827 --> 00:04:12,219
The middle class grew faster than at

125
00:04:12,219 --> 00:04:14,145
any time in American history.

126
00:04:14,325 --> 00:04:15,798
Families bought cars,

127
00:04:15,798 --> 00:04:19,304
televisions and washing machines at an astonishing

128
00:04:19,304 --> 00:04:19,654
rate.

129
00:04:20,004 --> 00:04:21,524
And a lot of that buying was

130
00:04:21,524 --> 00:04:22,338
done on credit.

131
00:04:22,518 --> 00:04:25,280
The Diners Club card appeared in nineteen

132
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:27,166
fifty, and by the end of the

133
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:29,995
decade, installment plans were everywhere.

134
00:04:30,175 --> 00:04:32,143
A family could take home a new

135
00:04:32,143 --> 00:04:34,571
refrigerator or a new Chevrolet for a

136
00:04:34,571 --> 00:04:36,802
few dollars down and a few dollars

137
00:04:36,802 --> 00:04:37,327
a month.

138
00:04:37,507 --> 00:04:40,089
Household debt grew quickly through the whole

139
00:04:40,089 --> 00:04:40,490
decade.

140
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:42,777
And yet, in the late fifties,

141
00:04:42,777 --> 00:04:45,248
more than one in five Americans lived

142
00:04:45,248 --> 00:04:46,784
below the poverty line,

143
00:04:46,784 --> 00:04:49,989
according to the government's own later figures.

144
00:04:50,169 --> 00:04:52,294
That was around forty million people.

145
00:04:52,474 --> 00:04:55,818
Poverty was especially deep in Appalachia,

146
00:04:55,818 --> 00:04:57,092
the rural South,

147
00:04:57,092 --> 00:05:00,357
Indian reservations and the inner cities.

148
00:05:00,706 --> 00:05:03,382
Most middle-class families in the new suburbs

149
00:05:03,382 --> 00:05:04,631
simply didn't see it.

150
00:05:04,811 --> 00:05:06,601
Michael Harrington's book,

151
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:07,841
The Other America,

152
00:05:07,841 --> 00:05:10,045
published in nineteen sixty-two,

153
00:05:10,045 --> 00:05:12,525
shocked many readers who had no idea

154
00:05:12,525 --> 00:05:15,142
how many of their fellow citizens were

155
00:05:15,142 --> 00:05:17,071
going without enough to eat.

156
00:05:17,251 --> 00:05:18,933
For millions of Americans,

157
00:05:18,933 --> 00:05:20,420
the boom never arrived.

158
00:05:20,770 --> 00:05:22,862
The fourth myth is that life in

159
00:05:22,862 --> 00:05:24,346
the fifties was safer.

160
00:05:24,526 --> 00:05:25,568
In some ways,

161
00:05:25,568 --> 00:05:26,930
it felt that way.

162
00:05:27,110 --> 00:05:28,805
Children roamed free.

163
00:05:28,985 --> 00:05:30,865
Doors stayed unlocked.

164
00:05:31,045 --> 00:05:33,493
Crime rates in many places were lower

165
00:05:33,493 --> 00:05:35,875
than they would be in later decades.

166
00:05:36,225 --> 00:05:37,552
But in other ways,

167
00:05:37,552 --> 00:05:40,057
daily life was far more dangerous.

168
00:05:40,237 --> 00:05:42,136
Cars had no seat belts,

169
00:05:42,136 --> 00:05:45,603
no airbags and steel dashboards that could

170
00:05:45,603 --> 00:05:46,924
kill in a crash.

171
00:05:47,104 --> 00:05:48,763
In nineteen fifty-five,

172
00:05:48,763 --> 00:05:51,143
the death rate on American roads,

173
00:05:51,143 --> 00:05:52,297
per mile driven,

174
00:05:52,297 --> 00:05:54,822
was several times higher than it is

175
00:05:54,822 --> 00:05:55,254
today.

176
00:05:55,604 --> 00:05:57,334
And then there were cigarettes.

177
00:05:57,514 --> 00:05:58,984
In the mid-fifties,

178
00:05:58,984 --> 00:06:01,768
close to half of all American adults

179
00:06:01,768 --> 00:06:02,309
smoked.

180
00:06:02,489 --> 00:06:05,763
Doctors appeared in cigarette advertisements.

181
00:06:05,943 --> 00:06:07,509
People smoked in offices,

182
00:06:07,509 --> 00:06:09,326
in restaurants, on airplanes,

183
00:06:09,326 --> 00:06:11,393
in hospitals and even in front of

184
00:06:11,393 --> 00:06:13,523
their children in the car with the

185
00:06:13,523 --> 00:06:14,651
windows rolled up.

186
00:06:14,831 --> 00:06:17,164
Almost nobody thought twice about it.

187
00:06:17,515 --> 00:06:20,809
Children rode bicycles without helmets and played

188
00:06:20,809 --> 00:06:23,296
with toys that would be banned today.

189
00:06:23,476 --> 00:06:24,962
And then there was polio.

190
00:06:25,142 --> 00:06:27,951
Every summer, parents lived in fear of

191
00:06:27,951 --> 00:06:28,173
it.

192
00:06:28,353 --> 00:06:30,314
In nineteen fifty-two alone,

193
00:06:30,314 --> 00:06:33,535
there were about fifty-eight thousand cases in

194
00:06:33,535 --> 00:06:34,796
the United States.

195
00:06:34,976 --> 00:06:38,135
Swimming pools and movie theaters closed when

196
00:06:38,135 --> 00:06:39,608
an outbreak hit town.

197
00:06:39,959 --> 00:06:43,290
When Jonas Salk's vaccine was declared safe

198
00:06:43,290 --> 00:06:46,855
and effective in April of nineteen fifty-five,

199
00:06:46,855 --> 00:06:49,721
church bells rang and people cried in

200
00:06:49,721 --> 00:06:50,651
the streets.

201
00:06:50,831 --> 00:06:52,278
Within a few years,

202
00:06:52,278 --> 00:06:54,790
polio cases dropped dramatically.

203
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:57,133
It was one of the happiest days

204
00:06:57,133 --> 00:06:58,110
of the decade,

205
00:06:58,110 --> 00:07:00,901
precisely because the fear before it had

206
00:07:00,901 --> 00:07:01,809
been so real.

207
00:07:02,159 --> 00:07:05,243
The fifth myth is that fifties teenagers

208
00:07:05,243 --> 00:07:08,174
were polite, obedient kids who went to

209
00:07:08,174 --> 00:07:10,564
sock hops and drank milkshakes.

210
00:07:10,744 --> 00:07:11,743
Many of them did.

211
00:07:11,923 --> 00:07:13,945
But the grown-ups of the fifties were

212
00:07:13,945 --> 00:07:16,404
convinced that teenagers were out of control.

213
00:07:16,754 --> 00:07:18,397
In nineteen fifty-four,

214
00:07:18,397 --> 00:07:21,325
the United States Senate held hearings on

215
00:07:21,325 --> 00:07:22,826
juvenile delinquency.

216
00:07:23,006 --> 00:07:26,242
Comic books were blamed for corrupting children,

217
00:07:26,242 --> 00:07:28,736
and the comic book industry created a

218
00:07:28,736 --> 00:07:30,691
strict code to censor itself.

219
00:07:30,871 --> 00:07:33,519
Parents worried about hot rods,

220
00:07:33,519 --> 00:07:36,422
switchblades, leather jackets and,

221
00:07:36,422 --> 00:07:38,557
above all, rock and roll.

222
00:07:38,908 --> 00:07:42,309
When Elvis Presley appeared on television in

223
00:07:42,309 --> 00:07:46,251
nineteen fifty-six, some ministers called his music

224
00:07:46,251 --> 00:07:49,575
sinful, and one famous broadcast showed him

225
00:07:49,575 --> 00:07:51,353
only from the waist up.

226
00:07:51,533 --> 00:07:52,631
To many parents,

227
00:07:52,631 --> 00:07:54,964
rock and roll sounded like the end

228
00:07:54,964 --> 00:07:56,061
of civilization.

229
00:07:56,241 --> 00:07:58,016
Their children, of course,

230
00:07:58,016 --> 00:07:59,585
loved every note of it.

231
00:07:59,935 --> 00:08:02,547
And fifties teenagers grew up fast.

232
00:08:02,727 --> 00:08:04,176
In nineteen fifty-six,

233
00:08:04,176 --> 00:08:06,943
the typical American woman married for the

234
00:08:06,943 --> 00:08:08,722
first time at about twenty,

235
00:08:08,722 --> 00:08:11,027
and many brides were still in their

236
00:08:11,027 --> 00:08:11,423
teens.

237
00:08:11,603 --> 00:08:14,038
Plenty of young couples had their first

238
00:08:14,038 --> 00:08:16,223
baby before they turned twenty-one.

239
00:08:16,403 --> 00:08:19,340
Adulthood didn't wait for anyone in those

240
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:19,770
years.

241
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:22,312
The sixth myth is that the fifties

242
00:08:22,312 --> 00:08:24,889
were a carefree time when nobody worried

243
00:08:24,889 --> 00:08:25,856
about anything.

244
00:08:26,036 --> 00:08:29,222
In reality, Americans lived under the shadow

245
00:08:29,222 --> 00:08:30,599
of the atomic bomb.

246
00:08:30,779 --> 00:08:33,637
The Soviet Union tested its first atomic

247
00:08:33,637 --> 00:08:35,637
bomb in nineteen forty-nine,

248
00:08:35,637 --> 00:08:37,924
and the fear of nuclear war hung

249
00:08:37,924 --> 00:08:39,567
over the entire decade.

250
00:08:39,918 --> 00:08:42,797
Schoolchildren practiced duck and cover drills,

251
00:08:42,797 --> 00:08:45,494
crouching under their desks with their hands

252
00:08:45,494 --> 00:08:46,535
over their heads.

253
00:08:46,715 --> 00:08:49,724
Some families built bomb shelters in their

254
00:08:49,724 --> 00:08:51,371
backyards or basements,

255
00:08:51,371 --> 00:08:53,879
stocked with canned food and water.

256
00:08:54,059 --> 00:08:56,499
Cities tested air raid sirens on a

257
00:08:56,499 --> 00:08:59,514
regular schedule, and every child knew the

258
00:08:59,514 --> 00:08:59,945
sound.

259
00:09:00,295 --> 00:09:03,489
Then, in October of nineteen fifty-seven,

260
00:09:03,489 --> 00:09:06,139
the Soviet Union launched Sputnik,

261
00:09:06,139 --> 00:09:09,022
the first satellite ever to orbit the

262
00:09:09,022 --> 00:09:09,490
Earth.

263
00:09:09,670 --> 00:09:12,611
Americans went outside at night to watch

264
00:09:12,611 --> 00:09:13,861
it pass overhead.

265
00:09:14,041 --> 00:09:16,889
Suddenly the country worried that it was

266
00:09:16,889 --> 00:09:19,309
falling behind, and within a year,

267
00:09:19,309 --> 00:09:22,157
Congress passed a law pouring money into

268
00:09:22,157 --> 00:09:24,792
science and math in American schools.

269
00:09:25,141 --> 00:09:27,267
There was also fear of a different

270
00:09:27,267 --> 00:09:27,580
kind.

271
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:29,095
During the Red Scare,

272
00:09:29,095 --> 00:09:31,640
people lost their jobs because of rumors

273
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:33,866
that they had communist sympathies.

274
00:09:34,046 --> 00:09:37,962
Teachers, actors, writers and government workers were

275
00:09:37,962 --> 00:09:38,923
investigated.

276
00:09:39,103 --> 00:09:41,958
Many Americans learned to be careful about

277
00:09:41,958 --> 00:09:42,977
what they said,

278
00:09:42,977 --> 00:09:43,793
and to whom.

279
00:09:44,142 --> 00:09:46,089
And the biggest myth of all is

280
00:09:46,089 --> 00:09:48,295
that the fifties were the same for

281
00:09:48,295 --> 00:09:48,879
everyone.

282
00:09:49,059 --> 00:09:51,599
For many white middle-class families,

283
00:09:51,599 --> 00:09:52,972
it was a golden age.

284
00:09:53,152 --> 00:09:54,917
But for Black Americans,

285
00:09:54,917 --> 00:09:56,681
especially in the South,

286
00:09:56,681 --> 00:09:58,446
segregation was the law.

287
00:09:58,626 --> 00:10:01,839
Separate schools, separate water fountains,

288
00:10:01,839 --> 00:10:03,781
separate seats on the bus.

289
00:10:04,130 --> 00:10:06,498
The fifties were also when that began

290
00:10:06,498 --> 00:10:07,137
to change.

291
00:10:07,317 --> 00:10:08,903
In nineteen fifty-four,

292
00:10:08,903 --> 00:10:11,591
the Supreme Court ruled in Brown versus

293
00:10:11,591 --> 00:10:14,830
Board of Education that segregated schools were

294
00:10:14,830 --> 00:10:16,002
unconstitutional.

295
00:10:16,182 --> 00:10:17,884
In nineteen fifty-five,

296
00:10:17,884 --> 00:10:20,325
Rosa Parks refused to give up her

297
00:10:20,325 --> 00:10:22,397
seat on a bus in Montgomery,

298
00:10:22,397 --> 00:10:25,505
Alabama, and the bus boycott that followed

299
00:10:25,505 --> 00:10:27,281
lasted more than a year.

300
00:10:27,630 --> 00:10:29,418
In nineteen fifty-seven,

301
00:10:29,418 --> 00:10:32,695
nine Black students walked into Central High

302
00:10:32,695 --> 00:10:34,333
School in Little Rock,

303
00:10:34,333 --> 00:10:37,685
Arkansas, protected by soldiers of the United

304
00:10:37,685 --> 00:10:38,579
States Army.

305
00:10:38,759 --> 00:10:41,179
The fifties weren't only an age of

306
00:10:41,179 --> 00:10:42,033
contentment.

307
00:10:42,213 --> 00:10:44,065
They were the beginning of one of

308
00:10:44,065 --> 00:10:46,648
the great moral struggles in American history.

309
00:10:46,997 --> 00:10:48,913
Women faced limits too.

310
00:10:49,093 --> 00:10:50,491
In much of the country,

311
00:10:50,491 --> 00:10:53,045
a married woman could have trouble opening

312
00:10:53,045 --> 00:10:55,112
a credit account or getting a loan

313
00:10:55,112 --> 00:10:57,058
without her husband's signature.

314
00:10:57,238 --> 00:11:00,089
Many employers fired women as soon as

315
00:11:00,089 --> 00:11:01,707
they became pregnant.

316
00:11:01,887 --> 00:11:04,587
Those rules would only begin to change

317
00:11:04,587 --> 00:11:06,647
in the sixties and seventies.

318
00:11:06,997 --> 00:11:09,944
So, was everything we remember about the

319
00:11:09,944 --> 00:11:11,049
fifties a myth?

320
00:11:11,229 --> 00:11:11,520
No.

321
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:13,499
A lot of it was real.

322
00:11:13,679 --> 00:11:16,257
The economy really did boom.

323
00:11:16,437 --> 00:11:19,126
Families really did buy houses on one

324
00:11:19,126 --> 00:11:19,780
paycheck.

325
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:22,120
Neighbors really did know each other.

326
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:25,307
Kids really did play outside until dark.

327
00:11:25,487 --> 00:11:28,354
Many people really were happier with less.

328
00:11:28,704 --> 00:11:32,095
A gallon of gas cost around twenty-nine

329
00:11:32,095 --> 00:11:32,617
cents.

330
00:11:32,797 --> 00:11:34,871
A movie ticket was well under a

331
00:11:34,871 --> 00:11:35,340
dollar.

332
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:37,828
A good state university could cost a

333
00:11:37,828 --> 00:11:39,560
few hundred dollars a year.

334
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:41,678
And a man with a high school

335
00:11:41,678 --> 00:11:44,309
diploma could often find a factory job

336
00:11:44,309 --> 00:11:46,732
that paid enough to support a whole

337
00:11:46,732 --> 00:11:47,217
family.

338
00:11:47,397 --> 00:11:49,080
Those numbers are not a myth.

339
00:11:49,430 --> 00:11:51,374
The truth is that the fifties were

340
00:11:51,374 --> 00:11:51,660
both.

341
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:54,073
A decade of hope and fear,

342
00:11:54,073 --> 00:11:55,962
of plenty and poverty,

343
00:11:55,962 --> 00:11:59,398
of tight communities and deep divisions.

344
00:11:59,578 --> 00:12:00,784
Like every decade,

345
00:12:00,784 --> 00:12:03,463
it was more complicated than the picture

346
00:12:03,463 --> 00:12:04,535
on the postcard.

347
00:12:04,715 --> 00:12:07,153
And that makes it more interesting,

348
00:12:07,153 --> 00:12:07,780
not less.

349
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:09,918
If you grew up in the fifties,

350
00:12:09,918 --> 00:12:11,288
your memories are true.

351
00:12:11,468 --> 00:12:12,946
The ones of the porch,

352
00:12:12,946 --> 00:12:15,834
the station wagon and the corner drugstore.

353
00:12:16,014 --> 00:12:18,283
They're just one part of a bigger,

354
00:12:18,283 --> 00:12:19,752
more surprising story.

355
00:12:19,932 --> 00:12:22,437
And that story is still being told,

356
00:12:22,437 --> 00:12:24,297
by everyone who was there.

357
00:12:24,647 --> 00:12:26,540
So tell us in the comments.

358
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:28,764
Which of these myths surprised you,

359
00:12:28,764 --> 00:12:30,750
and what do you think people today

360
00:12:30,750 --> 00:12:32,386
get wrong about the fifties?

361
00:12:32,566 --> 00:12:33,947
We read every comment.

362
00:12:34,127 --> 00:12:35,223
And stay with us,

363
00:12:35,223 --> 00:12:37,607
because next we're looking at what it

364
00:12:37,607 --> 00:12:40,443
was like to retire in nineteen seventy-five.

365
00:12:40,623 --> 00:12:42,309
Thank you for spending this time with

366
00:12:42,309 --> 00:12:42,446
me.
